Subject: [PATCH 05823/11362] [outer-loop] run the properly-scoped multi-seed
 TRM test: gate fails, deprioritizing

Ran the properly-scoped test designed after the two contradictory
single-seed pilots. Pre-registered before running (to avoid post-hoc
rationalization): 10 seeds per (game, framing, architecture)
combination, both framings (frame-only, frame+8-action-history), 5
held-out games spanning different size/imbalance characteristics (ft09,
m0r0, vc33, sk48, cd82). 200 total training runs, paired Wilcoxon
signed-rank test per combination. Falsifiable gate fixed in advance:
recursion supported only if p<0.05 in a majority (>=5 of 10)
combinations.

Result: the gate fails outright. 0 of 10 combinations reached
significance in recursion's favor. Recursion had a higher mean in only
2/10 combinations (neither significant); the baseline had a higher mean
in the other 8/10; the ONE combination that reached significance
anywhere in the sweep (sk48+history, p=0.049) favored the baseline, not
recursion.

This resolves the earlier contradiction cleanly: both single-seed
pilots were noise, not a real condition-dependent effect. With proper
statistical power, there is no reliable recursive advantage in this
setup.

Per the pre-registered commitment, this is deprioritized as scoped --
not because the broader TRM-as-generator idea is dead, but because this
toy-scale standalone reimplementation (4.2M params, no ACT halting, one
shared training recipe) doesn't show the effect on single-step action
classification. Explicitly does NOT invalidate the original Sudoku
precedent (a real, decisive effect using TRM's actual validated
architecture on a genuinely different, constraint-structured task) --
this test cannot distinguish whether the gap is architecture fidelity,
task structure, or training budget.

